==================================================
SINGLE LEADER REPLICATION — COMPLETE MASTER NOTES
==================================================

--------------------------------------------------
0. REAL LIFE INTUITION (VERY IMPORTANT)
--------------------------------------------------

Example 1: Google Docs (simplified model)
- Only ONE server decides final version (leader)
- Others just copy data

Example 2: Banking system
- All transactions go to ONE main database
- Other databases are just replicas

Example 3: Instagram
- When you post → goes to main server
- Feed reading → from replicas

CORE IDEA:
→ ONE authority for writes
→ MANY copies for reads

--------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS SINGLE LEADER REPLICATION?
--------------------------------------------------

DEFINITION:
- One node = LEADER (Primary)
- Other nodes = FOLLOWERS (Replicas)

RULE:
- ALL WRITES → go to LEADER
- FOLLOWERS → only replicate and serve reads

--------------------------------------------------
2. BASIC ARCHITECTURE (MENTAL MODEL)
--------------------------------------------------

Client → Leader (write)
              ↓
         Replication Log
              ↓
     Followers (apply changes)

Flow:
1. Client sends write
2. Leader updates data
3. Leader logs change (WAL/binlog)
4. Followers copy and apply changes

--------------------------------------------------
3. WHY DO WE USE SINGLE LEADER?
--------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
EXAMPLE: E-commerce (Amazon)
-------------------------------------

- Orders must be correct
- No two conflicting updates

WHY SINGLE LEADER:
- Avoids conflicts
- Ensures order of writes

-------------------------------------
BENEFITS
-------------------------------------

1. SIMPLE DESIGN
- One authority → easy reasoning

2. STRONG CONSISTENCY (if sync)
- No conflicting writes

3. NO CONFLICT RESOLUTION NEEDED
- Leader decides everything

4. EASY DEBUGGING
- All writes centralized

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFFS
-------------------------------------

- Leader = bottleneck
- Single point of failure
- Write scalability limited

--------------------------------------------------
4. HOW DATA FLOWS (DEEP UNDERSTANDING)
--------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
STEP-BY-STEP FLOW
-------------------------------------

1. Client sends write request
2. Leader:
    - Updates DB
    - Writes to WAL (Write Ahead Log)
3. Leader sends log to followers
4. Followers replay log

-------------------------------------
IMPORTANT INSIGHT
-------------------------------------

Followers do NOT execute random queries
→ They just REPLAY exact changes

This ensures consistency

--------------------------------------------------
5. SYNCHRONOUS VS ASYNCHRONOUS (IN THIS MODEL)
--------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
SYNC MODE
-------------------------------------

- Leader waits for followers before ACK

Example:
Bank transfer → must confirm everywhere

+ Strong consistency
- Slow

-------------------------------------
ASYNC MODE
-------------------------------------

- Leader responds immediately
- Followers update later

Example:
Instagram post

+ Fast
- Stale reads possible

--------------------------------------------------
6. FAILURE SCENARIOS (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)
--------------------------------------------------

==================================================
6.1 FOLLOWER GOES DOWN
==================================================

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

- Instagram has 5 replicas
- One replica crashes

-------------------------------------
WHAT HAPPENS?
-------------------------------------

- Leader still works
- Other followers still serve reads
- System continues normally

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

- That follower is OUTDATED
- Misses updates

-------------------------------------
WHEN IT COMES BACK?
-------------------------------------

- It must CATCH UP

-------------------------------------
HOW?
-------------------------------------

1. Read replication log (WAL)
2. Replay missing updates

-------------------------------------
EDGE CASE
-------------------------------------

If logs deleted:
→ Full resync needed (expensive)

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ System stays available
- Recovery cost
- Possible stale reads

==================================================
6.2 LEADER GOES DOWN
==================================================

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

- Main DB crashes during Amazon checkout

-------------------------------------
WHAT HAPPENS?
-------------------------------------

- No writes possible ❌
- Reads may still work (from followers)

-------------------------------------
BIG PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

System becomes READ-ONLY

-------------------------------------
NEED:
-------------------------------------

Leader Election (failover)

-------------------------------------
TEMPORARY ISSUES
-------------------------------------

- Downtime
- Data loss (if async replication)

Example:
Write accepted by leader but not yet replicated
→ Leader crashes
→ That write LOST

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Simplicity
- Availability issue during failure

(NOTE: Election mechanism deep me baad me aata hai)

==================================================
6.3 SPLIT BRAIN (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥🔥)
==================================================

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE ANALOGY
-------------------------------------

Think 2 captains on one ship:
- Both giving different orders
→ Chaos

-------------------------------------
WHAT IS SPLIT BRAIN?
-------------------------------------

Two nodes think they are LEADER at same time

-------------------------------------
HOW IT HAPPENS?
-------------------------------------

Network partition:

Leader A         Followers B,C
   |                  |
---Network Break------
   |                  |

Now:
- A thinks: "I am leader"
- B,C elect new leader

→ TWO LEADERS

-------------------------------------
REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

User A writes to Leader1:
    balance = 100

User B writes to Leader2:
    balance = 200

Now:
→ Data conflict 💥

-------------------------------------
WHY THIS IS DANGEROUS?
-------------------------------------

- Data corruption
- Inconsistent state
- Hard to recover

-------------------------------------
SOLUTIONS (HIGH LEVEL)
-------------------------------------

1. QUORUM BASED ELECTION
- Majority decides leader

2. FENCING TOKENS
- Old leader rejected

3. LEASE MECHANISM
- Leader valid only for time period

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Prevents corruption
- Adds complexity
- Requires coordination

--------------------------------------------------
7. BIG PICTURE FLOW (CONNECT EVERYTHING)
--------------------------------------------------

Normal Flow:
Client → Leader → Followers

Failure Flow:
Follower down → recover via logs
Leader down → election needed
Network partition → split brain risk

